{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And it was so.</b> Its meaning: so it was as it is now, above the firmament. <b>And it was so</b> should have been written immediately after 'between water and water.' But had that been so, I would say that the separation was the completion of the work of the waters, and it did not occur until the third day, when the waters were gathered to one place. Rashi wrote: why is 'that it was good' not said on the second day? Because the work of the waters was not yet completed etc. Even so, since the work of separating the waters by means of the firmament was completed on it, and it appears as though its work was completed on it, Scripture writes concerning it 'and it was evening and it was morning.' Our Rabbis said: one does not begin on bet, and the reason is — just as with the work of Creation, a matter begun on it had its completion delayed until the next day, so too anything one begins on it is delayed in completion. And likewise they say: one does not conclude on dalet, and the reason is because on it all the affairs of the world were completed — only, from there onward all living creatures were created, and once the Holy One, blessed be He, completed the structures of the world, He never again began another on it. Why 'that it was good' was not said on the second day: because on it the light of Gehenna was created, as is stated in the chapter 'A place where they were accustomed'; and the Holy One, blessed be He, does not rejoice in the downfall of His creatures, as it is written: ובצאתו לפני החלוץ אומר הודו לה׳ כי לעולם חסדו. And our Rabbis said: 'that it was good' was not said in this thanksgiving because the Holy One, blessed be He, does not rejoice in the downfall of the wicked. And Rabbi Elazar said: He went back and included it in the sixth day, as it is written: והנה טוב מאד."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:18:58.405511990Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}